Three complicating factors appear in this application: parallel computation, the ALE 9Adaptive Lagrangian±Eulerian) treatment, and different response scales. The in ̄uence of parallelism is discussed in Section 6.5. The second complication arises because the structure motions are described in a Lagrangian system, in which the structural mesh follows its motion, whereas  ̄uid  ̄ow is described in a Eulerian system, in which the gas passes through the  ̄uid mesh. Hence the  ̄uid mesh, or at least a near-®eld portion of it, must displace in lockstep with the structural motions. There are many proposed ALE solutions that work well in two dimensions, but which often lose robustness in three dimensions because of mesh cell collapse. The approach selected in this project exploits an idea originally proposed by Batina [40]. A ®ctitious network of linear springs, which may be augmented by dampers and torsional springs, is laid down along the edges of the  ̄uid volume elements, as sketched in Fig.139b). This network may be viewed as a coupled computational ®eld embedded within the  ̄uid partition. The springs are ®xed at the outer edges of the region where ALE e ects are deemed important. They are driven by the motion of the aircraft surface, and operate as transducers that feed this motion, appropriately decaying with distance, to the  ̄uid mesh nodes. The motion of the dynamic mesh must satisfy geometric conservation laws [37]. The resulting three-®eld interaction is diagramed in Fig. 13. Although the diagram is topologically similar to the three-®eld cavitating FSI of Fig. 119b), the nature of  ̄ow computations makes this a more challenging problem. In commercial aircraft aeroelasticity, structural motions are typically dominated by low frequency vibration modes. On the other hand, the  ̄uid response must be captured in a smaller time scale because of non-stationary e ects involving shocks, vortices, and turbulence. Thus the use of a smaller timestep for the  ̄uid is natural. This device is called subcycling. The ratio of structural to  ̄uid timesteps may range from 10:1 through as high as 1000:1, depending on problem characteristics and the use of explicit or implicit  ̄uid solver.
